  "captionStyle": "nick-display",
  "script": "Apple just skipped the iPhone 18.\nFor the first time in 7 years, there's no standard iPhone this September.\nPegatron \u2014 one of the companies that actually builds iPhones \u2014 just confirmed it on this week's earnings call.\nApple is splitting the iPhone 18 launch in two.\nWave 1, September 2026: the iPhone 18 Pro, the 18 Pro Max \u2014 and reportedly Apple's first foldable, the iPhone Ultra.\nWave 2, March 2027: the standard iPhone 18, the budget 18e, and the iPhone Air 2.\nEvery number iPhone from the iPhone 11 to the iPhone 17 launched in September. 7 generations straight.\nSo why break it now? Simply put, September becomes a pure premium show.\nPro models carry Apple's fattest margins, and they get the entire holiday quarter to themselves.\nOn top of it, Pegatron says component shortages \u2014 chips and memory \u2014 could run into 2028.\nAnd look, if parts are scarce, you don't want 6 new iPhones fighting for them at once.\nAnyways, this has been building for a year \u2014 analyst Ming-Chi Kuo called a spring 2027 iPhone 18 months ago.\nIt's just the first time the supply chain said it out loud.\nTo be fair, Apple hasn't confirmed a thing. And in leaks like this, dates are always the softest detail.\nIf it sticks, the iPhone becomes a twice-a-year event.\nSo \u2014 wait till March, or is Apple betting you'll just buy the Pro?\nTell me in the comments.\n",
